import DocsApiTable from './DocsApiTable.astro';
---
  <p>Import <code>bindEffect</code> from <code>@domfx/gsap</code>. It accepts any live, externally controlled <code>EffectHandle</code>. Supply the effect instance, a caller-owned GSAP timeline and these options:</p>
  <DocsApiTable label="GSAP binding options">
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Property</th><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Default</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>time</code><p>Absolute destination time in seconds. Must be finite and representable as float32.</p></th><td><code>number</code></td><td>Required</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>intensity</code><p>Finite destination strength. Clamped to 0–1 before interpolation.</p></th><td><code>number</code></td><td>Required</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>duration</code><p>Finite, nonnegative interpolation duration in seconds. Zero is accepted.</p></th><td><code>number</code></td><td>Required</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>position</code><p>GSAP insertion position. Omitting it appends to the timeline.</p></th><td><code>gsap.Position</code></td><td><code>undefined</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>disposeEffect</code><p>Give effect cleanup responsibility to the binding, including context revert.</p></th><td><code>boolean</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </DocsApiTable>
  <p><code>bindEffect(effect, timeline, options)</code> returns a <code>EffectBinding</code>. It snapshots starting controls and interpolates linearly. <code>binding.dispose()</code> removes its tween and an explicitly owned attachment; the caller still owns the timeline.</p>
  <p><code>bindEffectAsync(promise, timeline, options)</code> accepts a promise of any effect handle and requires <code>disposeEffect: true</code>. It returns an <code>AsyncEffectBinding</code> with <code>dispose()</code> and <code>ready: Promise&lt;void&gt;</code>, releasing late results after cancellation. Use <code>context.revert()</code> for GSAP context cleanup.</p>
  <p>See the <a href="/docs/gsap">GSAP guide</a> for a complete sequence and teardown.</p>
  <p><code>bindFire</code>, <code>bindFireAsync</code> and the Fire-named types remain aliases for compatibility. Both names preserve the same snapshot behavior.</p>
  <p class="docs-api-note">These helpers are adapters, not registered GSAP property plugins. They animate private proxy targets: <code>gsap.killTweensOf(effect)</code> and cross-binding overwrite do not address their children. Multiple bindings created together snapshot the same starting controls; use native <code>.to()</code> calls for normal chained sequencing.</p>
  <p>Killing the caller's timeline alone does not remove a binding's disposal subscription. Dispose the binding too. An asynchronous binding's <code>ready</code> waits for binding setup or late-result cleanup, not the effect's own initial setup.</p>
